<!-- ============================================================
     Langflow CVE-2026-0770: Unauthenticated RCE via Code Validation
     Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere (CWE-829)

     Threat:
       Remote code execution (CVSS 9.8 CRITICAL, CVSSv4 8.9 HIGH) in
       Langflow versions prior to 1.3.0. The validate_code() function
       in src/lfx/src/lfx/custom/validate.py calls Python exec() on
       user-supplied code without sandboxing. An attacker POSTs a
       crafted Python function to /api/v1/validate/code; Python
       evaluates default argument expressions at function definition
       time within exec(), triggering immediate OS command execution.
       Command output is exfiltrated via raised exceptions in the HTTP
       error response body (prefix "OUTPUT:").

       CISA KEV: Added July 21, 2026. Federal deadline July 24, 2026.
       Public PoC (EDB-52597, GitHub: 0xgh057r3c0n, affix, diamorphine666)
       available since May 2026; mass exploitation began within hours.

     Attack flow:
       1. (Optional) POST /api/v1/login with default credentials
          username=langflow password=langflow when AUTO_LOGIN is enabled,
          to obtain a JWT for the authenticated code injection path.
       2. POST /api/v1/validate/code with JSON {"code": "<payload>"}
          where <payload> is a Python function using default argument
          expressions that embed __import__('subprocess').run().
       3. exec() compiles the function definition, evaluating default
          args and running the OS command immediately.
       4. Generator .throw() raises an exception carrying "OUTPUT:\n
          <command_result>", which propagates as an HTTP 5xx error body.

     Exploit kernel (simplified from EDB-52597):
       {"code": "def x(_=(lambda r: (_ for _ in ()).throw(
         Exception(f'OUTPUT:{r.stdout}')))(
         __import__('subprocess').run('id',
           shell=True, capture_output=True))):\n  pass"}

     Post-exploitation:
       Active campaigns (related CVE-2026-33017, Trend Micro / SOC Prime
       July 2026) deploy XMRig Monero cryptominers via stratum+tcp pools
       and steal AI API credentials (.env, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY,
       .aws/credentials). auditd-based post-exploitation indicators are
       partially covered by existing Langflow rules 101710-101711 and 103917;
       see also 103915 for AI API key file access detection.

     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application
       T1059.006 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Python
       T1552.001 - Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Files
       T1496     - Resource Hijacking (cryptominer deployment)

     Rule IDs: 104121-104127
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Issue: ESKA-610
     Date: 2026-07-21

     Log source prerequisites:
       All rules (web/access log detection):
         Traefik access log in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Required fields: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost,
         DownstreamStatus, ClientHost.
         Decoder: built-in Wazuh JSON decoder (decoded_as=json).
       No host-agent rules in this file; post-exploitation coverage
       provided by existing Langflow auditd rules 101710, 101711,
       103917 (Python subprocess spawning from web process parent).

     Severity tiers:
       0  - POST to /api/v1/validate/code (anchor, chains to children)
       12 - POST to validate/code (may be legitimate dev use)
       15 - POST to validate/code returned HTTP 2xx (exec() succeeded)
       14 - POST to validate/code returned HTTP 5xx (exec() exception;
              consistent with generator .throw() payload exfiltrating
              OS command output as "OUTPUT:" in error body)
       13 - 3+ requests from same IP in 60 s (scanner/automated tool)
       0  - POST to /api/v1/login (anchor for chain correlation)
       14 - Login followed by validate/code from same IP within 120 s
              (complete default-credential auth bypass + RCE chain)

     References:
       CISA KEV Catalog:
         https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog
       NVD CVE-2026-0770:
         https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-0770
       GitHub Security Advisory (GHSA-v8hw-mh8c-jxfc):
         https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/security/advisories/GHSA-v8hw-mh8c-jxfc
       GitHub Security Advisory (GHSA-vwmf-pq79-vjvx):
         https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/security/advisories/GHSA-vwmf-pq79-vjvx
       Exploit-DB EDB-52597:
         https://www.exploit-db.com/exploits/52597
       Trend Micro - CVE-2026-33017 Cryptominer Campaign:
         https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/research/26/f/from-langflow-to-monero-inside-cve-2026-33017-cryptominer.html
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1190:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1059.006:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/006/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,execution,langflow,cve_2026_0770,cisa_kev,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104121: Base anchor - POST to /api/v1/validate/code
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       Level 0: no alert; chains to rules 104122-104125.

       /api/v1/validate/code is the sole exploitation vector for
       CVE-2026-0770. The endpoint accepts user-supplied Python code
       and passes it directly to validate_code() which calls exec()
       for syntax and structure validation. Legitimate use exists
       only during custom component development; production deployments
       that do not expose the Langflow IDE should have no traffic on
       this path. All hits on this anchor warrant investigation.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104121" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/api/v1/validate/code\b</field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770: POST to /api/v1/validate/code from $(ClientHost) (base anchor, chains to 104122-104125)</description>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,code_validation,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104122: POST to /api/v1/validate/code - endpoint monitor
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)
       Child of rule 104121.

       Any POST to /api/v1/validate/code is a potential CVE-2026-0770
       exploitation attempt and should be reviewed by analysts. The
       endpoint calls exec() on the request body; no additional payload
       pattern is required to achieve RCE. Level 12: suspicious in
       production environments, especially from external or unexpected
       source IPs. Correlate with response code rules 104123-104124
       and login chain rule 104127.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104122" level="12">
    <if_sid>104121</if_sid>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770: POST to /api/v1/validate/code from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost) - code validation endpoint accessed; CVE-2026-0770 achieves RCE by passing exec()-evaluated Python to this endpoint; CVSS 9.8 pre-auth; review source IP and response code (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,code_validation,exploit_attempt,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104123: HTTP 2xx response to POST /api/v1/validate/code
       MITRE: T1190, T1059.006
       Child of rule 104122.

       A successful HTTP 2xx response means exec() compiled and ran
       the submitted Python code without raising an unhandled
       exception. In the CVE-2026-0770 exploitation context this can
       indicate: (a) the attacker submitted benign code to confirm
       endpoint availability before the real payload, or (b) the
       exec() payload ran without raising a detectable exception
       (e.g. used os.system() side-effects rather than .throw()).
       Either case warrants immediate investigation of the Langflow
       host for child process spawning and outbound connections.
       Level 15: initiate incident response.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104123" level="15">
    <if_sid>104122</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770 CRITICAL: POST to /api/v1/validate/code returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost) - exec() accepted and evaluated submitted code; possible silent RCE via Python default argument injection; initiate incident response: inspect Langflow host for subprocess spawning (rules 101710, 103917), outbound connections, cryptominer activity, and .env or AI API key access (T1190, T1059.006)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,rce_possible,execution_success,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104124: HTTP 5xx response to POST /api/v1/validate/code
       MITRE: T1190, T1059.006
       Child of rule 104122.

       The canonical CVE-2026-0770 PoC (EDB-52597) uses Python's
       generator .throw() mechanism to raise an exception whose
       message contains "OUTPUT:\n<os command result>". exec()
       propagates this exception to the Langflow API handler, which
       returns an HTTP 5xx Internal Server Error with the exception
       body. A 5xx from /api/v1/validate/code is therefore the
       primary HTTP-layer fingerprint of exploit payload execution
       and command output exfiltration in the response body.
       Level 14: high-confidence exploit attempt; investigate host.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104124" level="14">
    <if_sid>104122</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^5\d{2}$</field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770: POST to /api/v1/validate/code returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost) - server-side exception from exec(); consistent with EDB-52597 generator .throw() payload; command output likely exfiltrated as "OUTPUT:" in error body; treat as active RCE attempt and audit Langflow host for subprocess spawning and outbound connections (T1190, T1059.006)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,rce_attempt,exploit_exception,high_fidelity,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104125: Scanner - 3+ requests to validate/code in 60 s
       MITRE: T1190
       Frequency rule on base anchor 104121.

       Automated exploit frameworks and mass-scanning tools probe
       /api/v1/validate/code with rapid repeated requests: first to
       confirm endpoint availability, then to iterate over payload
       variants. Active CVE-2026-0770 campaigns have been observed
       using python-requests/2.25.1 as the user agent. Three or
       more requests from the same source within 60 seconds is
       inconsistent with interactive developer use and indicates
       automated tooling. Recommend immediate firewall block on
       the source IP and inspection of all prior requests from it.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104125" level="13" frequency="3" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104121</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770: 3+ POST requests to /api/v1/validate/code from $(ClientHost) within 60 s - automated exploit scanner probing CVE-2026-0770 RCE endpoint on $(RequestHost); block source IP at perimeter and inspect prior requests for successful exec() responses (T1190)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,scanner,automated_attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104126: Base anchor - POST to /api/v1/login
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       Level 0: no alert; used for correlation in rule 104127.

       POST /api/v1/login is the Langflow authentication endpoint.
       Default installations ship with credentials username=langflow
       password=langflow when AUTO_LOGIN is enabled. Public PoC tools
       for CVE-2026-0770 attempt these default credentials to obtain
       a JWT before posting the exec() payload to validate/code. This
       anchor fires on all login attempts; rule 104127 provides the
       high-confidence correlation: login followed by validate/code
       from the same source within 120 seconds.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104126" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/api/v1/login\b</field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770: POST to /api/v1/login from $(ClientHost) (base anchor for unauth RCE chain correlation, chains to 104127)</description>
    <group>langflow,auth,cve_2026_0770,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104127: Login then validate/code from same IP within 120 s
       MITRE: T1190, T1059.006
       Correlation: if_matched_sid 104126, current event 104121.

       The documented CVE-2026-0770 unauthenticated RCE chain from
       public PoC tools (EDB-52597, 0xgh057r3c0n, affix) is:
         1. POST /api/v1/login with langflow:langflow (rule 104126)
            to obtain a JWT from an AUTO_LOGIN-enabled instance.
         2. POST /api/v1/validate/code with exec() RCE payload
            (rule 104121 fires for the validate/code request).
       Correlation of both events from the same source IP within
       120 seconds is high-confidence default-credential auth bypass
       followed by Python exec() code injection. Level 14: initiate
       incident response immediately; isolate Langflow host and rotate
       all credentials stored in the deployment environment.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104127" level="14" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104126</if_matched_sid>
    <if_sid>104121</if_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>Langflow CVE-2026-0770 EXPLOITATION CHAIN: $(ClientHost) POSTed to /api/v1/login then /api/v1/validate/code on $(RequestHost) within 2 min - high-confidence default-credential auth bypass followed by Python exec() RCE; canonical attack chain from EDB-52597 PoC; isolate Langflow host immediately, block source IP, audit for subprocess spawning and .env or AI API key exfiltration (T1190, T1059.006)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>langflow,cve_2026_0770,exploitation_chain,auth_bypass,rce,critical,high_fidelity,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
